From a51b11e59b1f61b3950f81c5bb14610c08e02560 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 12:51:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 01/10] D-51 (R4.5, M-PHYSICS-024 b): launch sites on WGS-84,
 one height datum
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The owner decided on 2026-10-17 ("ทำพร้อมกัน", docs/DECISIONS.md D-51) that
the launch sites move onto the real Earth shape together with J2 in the
point-mass ascent (next commit). This commit is the WGS-84 half.

- groundPositionEci places a site at its geodetic latitude and its altitude
  above the WGS-84 ellipsoid (geodesy.ts geodeticToCartesian): 11.0 km lower
  in radius at Baikonur, 16.9 km at Plesetsk, 13.2 km at Vostochny, 4.9 km at
  the Cape. eciToLatLon returns the geodetic latitude and height, so a pad
  reads back its own latitude.
- One height rule. The air (density, pressure, wind, tail-off back-pressure),
  the ground (contact, impact, landing burns, settling, groundElevation) and
  every height shown (SimState.altitude/lat, events' alt) read the height
  above WGS-84. Orbit heights (apsides, insertion targets, the closed loop's
  terminal altitude, the cut-off checks) stay radius - 6,378.137 km (GCAT).
  The guidance gets the WGS-84 height for its atmospheric gates (pitch-over
  AGL, gravity-turn hand-over and blend, the explicit laws' engage height).
- The planner's orbit-plane geometry (azimuth, expected node, launch windows,
  corridor reach) uses the pad's geocentric latitude (padPlaneLatitude).
- The hold-down release weighs the stack with the J2 field and the Earth's
  turn at the pad: Somigliana's normal gravity.
- DescentCapsule.datum is retired: every return reads WGS-84 heights;
  VOSTOK_CAPSULE keeps `onLand` for the steppe the scene draws.
- Apollo: only the air and the sea at the splash move onto WGS-84 (the
  entry interface stays on the radius its targeting uses).

Every fingerprint moves; the named re-records follow in their own commit.

